<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/conflicts/6th-century-bce">Conflicts &amp; Resolutions</a> · Battle</p><h1 class="enc-title">Battle of Opis</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/539-bce">539 BCE</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> was fought in 539 BCE near the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mesopotamia">Mesopotamia</a>, where the Persian forces of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-the-great">Cyrus the Great</a> defeated the Babylonian army. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> itself then fell without meaningful resistance. The victory absorbed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a> into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire</a> and was followed by the issuing of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a>.</p></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> stood on the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a> about 80 kilometres north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baghdad">Baghdad</a> and is thought to have been a favoured crossing point; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> describes a bridge there. It also lay at one end of the Median Wall, a defensive barrier north of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> built decades earlier by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nebuchadnezzar-ii">Nebuchadnezzar II</a>, so its capture would open the road to the capital. The invasion may have been timed for early autumn, when the rivers are at their lowest.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylonia">Babylonia</a>, bordered by the Persian empire to the north, east and west, suffered severe economic problems made worse by plague and famine, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> was said to be unpopular for his unconventional religious policies. Cyrus is said to have won over Gobryas, governor of the frontier region of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>, which served as the starting point of the invasion. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a> records that Nabonidus had cult statues brought into the capital beforehand, so the fighting may have begun in the winter of 540 BCE.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus-chronicle">Nabonidus Chronicle</a> places the battle in the month of Tashritu at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> on the bank of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tigris">Tigris</a>, between Cyrus and the army of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/akkad">Akkad</a>, but gives no detail of its course, the forces or the casualties. The Babylonian commander is not named: some assume it was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/belshazzar">Belshazzar</a>, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, while Paul-Alain Beaulieu holds that Nabonidus himself led. The defeated army is not mentioned again, which suggests a rout. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took plunder, and most translations mention a massacre of the people of Akkad, though translators disagree on who was killed and by whom; Amélie Kuhrt sees a probably hard-won victory, while W. G. Lambert argues there was no massacre at all.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>According to the chronicle, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sippar">Sippar</a> was captured without battle on 6 October and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a> fled. On 12 October Ug/Gubaru, governor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gutium">Gutium</a>, entered <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> with the army of Cyrus without a battle, and Nabonidus was captured on his return. The chronicle notes that the rites of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/esagila">Esagila</a> and the other temples were not interrupted. Cyrus entered Babylon on 29 October and was proclaimed king.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder</a> does not mention the battle and presents Cyrus as liberating <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> peacefully, but <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/opis">Opis</a> shows that the Babylonian regime resisted the invasion. The later Greek accounts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/herodotus">Herodotus</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/xenophon">Xenophon</a> tell of a siege and the diverting of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/euphrates">Euphrates</a>, which neither the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuneiform">cuneiform</a> sources nor the city's archaeology support.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Battle of Opis” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 539 BCE Cyrus defeated the Babylonian army at the <b>Battle of Opis</b> and entered Babylon, absorbing the Neo-Babylonian Empire with little resistance.</blockquote><a href="/empire/achaemenid-empire">Achaemenid Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The text also omits events that other sources record, among them the <b>Battle of Opis</b>, and historians such as Lisbeth Fried and Max Mallowan read its account of a peaceful welcome as conqueror's rhetoric.</blockquote><a href="/work/cyrus-cylinder">Cyrus Cylinder · Legacy</a></li></ul></section>
